From 8ae3d5873d784aca2535cab09359c873a5709f57 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jacob Root <me@rooty.dev>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 03:30:07 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 5/9] base: place an arena file mapping without leaving a hole
 on failure

MemoryMappingArena placed a file mapping with a single mmap(MAP_FIXED)
over the arena. mmap(MAP_FIXED) unmaps the target range before it calls
the file's own mmap operation, so a descriptor whose file cannot be
mapped (a pipe or a socket), a bad file offset, or a writable request on
a read-only descriptor fails *after* the reservation there is gone,
leaving a hole. crosvm's vhost-user shared-memory arena is covered by a
KVM memory slot, so an unrelated mmap that later lands in that hole
becomes memory the guest reads and writes through the slot -- a
guest-to-VMM exposure a compromised (or buggy) vhost-user backend can
steer into by handing over such a descriptor.

Place the fd at an address the kernel picks first, where a failure leaves
the arena untouched, then move it over the target atomically with
mremap(MREMAP_FIXED). Should the move itself fail after it has unmapped
the destination, restore the arena's reservation over the range as
PROT_NONE so a stray access faults here rather than reading whatever
lands next -- with MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE, so as never to replace a mapping
another thread made in the hole meanwhile, and checked: if the range
cannot be refilled, the process aborts rather than leave something
unknown behind a KVM slot. (A move the kernel refuses before unmapping
anything, at the mapping limit, aborts too: the two cannot be told apart
without trusting /proc/self/maps.) A hugetlbfs file is refused before
anything is mapped: its mapping is a whole huge page long, and moving it
into the arena would replace up to a gigabyte past the checked range.
Anonymous fixed mappings -- the path `remove` uses to re-reserve on unmap
-- have no such failure-after-unmap and are left as they were. Tests force
the pipe-fd failure and assert, via /proc/self/maps, that the arena keeps
every page; refill a hole and refuse to replace what is there; and refuse
a hugetlbfs file.

